---
import './keys.css';
const parts = [['product','Operation','Build a product card'],['v1','Representation','Read the version-1 format'],['north','Shop','Use north’s catalogue'],['P7','Product','Choose this shop’s P7'],['en-GB','Locale','Request British English'],['GBP','Currency','Request pounds sterling']];
---
<figure class="key-figure">
 <div class="figure-heading"><span class="figure-number">Figure 1</span>One key, one kind of answer</div>
 <div class="scene">
  <code>product:v1:north:P7:en-GB:GBP</code>
  <div class="parts">{parts.map(([value,label,meaning]) => <div class="box"><div class="label">{label}</div><strong><code>{value}</code></strong><span>{meaning}</span></div>)}</div>
  <div class="box"><strong>Blue mug · GBP 20.00</strong><span>Source product revision 1. Public catalogue card.</span></div>
  <p class="result">Another request ID changes none of these parts. Another shop, language or currency asks for a different representation.</p>
 </div>
 <figcaption>The invented shop example, with source state held fixed. The source supplies the requested representation; the cache stores it. The v1 field describes its format, not how recently P7 changed.</figcaption>
</figure>
